content:
Looking at the evidence, the rollback at t=480 (E11) did not resolve the issue — errors continue at 2.00/s through t=540 (E3, E7, E9). The "provider request failed" with status=503 (E9) points to the external payment provider (E15, E16). I need to check if the payment provider is still returning 503s post-rollback, and whether there's a database connection issue contributing.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "logs",
      "service": "payment-service",
      "level": "ERROR",
      "contains": "provider request failed",
      "why": "Confirm 503 errors from payment-provider are still occurring after the rollback at t=480, distinguishing external_dependency from residual bad_deploy effects."
    },
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT service, open FROM db_sessions WHERE service = 'payment-service'",
      "why": "Check if payment-service holds an abnormal number of open DB sessions, which could indicate resource_exhaustion rather than an external provider failure."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "service": "payment-provider",
      "kind": "external_dependency",
      "mechanism": "The external payment provider has been returning HTTP 503 errors since t=474, causing payment-service to fail all payment captures; the rollback did not fix this because the provider is outside our control.",
      "supporting": ["E9", "E7", "E6", "E3", "E15", "E16"],
      "refuting": ["E10", "E11"],
      "explains": ["I7_payments_succeed", "M2_error_burst", "E3", "E6", "E7", "E8"],
      "repair_plan": []
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The payment provider has been returning 503s since t=474 (pre-dating and surviving the rollback), indicating an ongoing external dependency failure that requires escalation."
}
```
